e_sha256" "392b915a99380ace9228f788a7719a263f5dcadabae869f0043172d69b625324"} {"question" "q-344824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/state.json" "file_sha256" "392b915a99380ace9228f788a7719a263f5dcadabae869f0043172d69b625324"} {"question" "q-344824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36ab8ad905d389b3fd0da5a3e3b8977c537e14fc3349094b89e3a06767065a83"} {"question" "q-344824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/state.json" "file_sha256" "392b915a99380ace9228f788a7719a263f5dcadabae869f0043172d69b625324"} {"question" "q-344824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/state.json" "file_sha256" "392b915a99380ace9228f788a7719a263f5dcadabae869f0043172d69b625324"} {"question" "q-344824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/state.json" "file_sha256" "392b915a99380ace9228f788a7719a263f5dcadabae869f0043172d69b625324"} {"question" "e-21423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\ny''' - 3y'' - 6y' + 8y = 2 + 5e^{-x}, \\qquad \\big\\{ y_1 = e^{-2x}, \\: y_2 = e^{x}, \\: y_3 = e^{4x} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)e^{-2x} +u_2(x)e^{x} + u_3(x)e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $u_1(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/example-21423.json" "file_sha256" "974c4e28c69ffb9863f6b5f2cf1da3b2025d09dd9c6dd32bf152fae457266456"} {"question" "e-21423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3},\n\\qquad\n{u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int{{\\frac{{g(x){W_i}(x)}}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x}}, \n\\qquad\ni=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x) = 2 + 5e^{-x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= e^{ - 2x}, \\qquad y_2= e^{x}, \\qquad y_3= e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 16{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{4x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 16\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}[1(16-4)-1(-32-16)+1(-2-4)] \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(12+48-6) \\\\  & =54{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find $u_1,$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & ={W}_{1}({y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ {e}^{x} & 4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4{e}^{5x}-{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(2+5{e}^{-x})(3{e}^{5x})}{54{e}^{3x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\int (2+5{e}^{-x}){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\int (2{e}^{2x}+5{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}({e}^{2x}+5{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/example-21423.json" "file_sha256" "974c4e28c69ffb9863f6b5f2cf1da3b2025d09dd9c6dd32bf152fae457266456"} {"question" "e-21429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\ny''' + 4y'  =  6,  \\qquad \\big\\{ y_1 = 1, \\: y_2 = \\cos(2x), \\: y_3 = \\sin(2x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x) = A + B \\cos(2x) + C \\sin(2x) + u_1(x) + u_2(x) \\cos(2x) + u_3(x) \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $u_3(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/example-21429.json" "file_sha256" "c93b800e4497786b37b64fe683196794289366a05dc23cea1e63eed9c913424f"} {"question" "e-21429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3},\n\\qquad\n{u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int \\frac{g(x){W_i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x, \n\\qquad\ni=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x) = 6.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= 1, \\qquad y_2= \\cos(2x), \\qquad y_3= \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+8{cos}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find $u_3,$ we compute the determinant $W_3(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071673/example-21429.json" "file_sha256" "c93b800e4497786b37b64fe683196794289366a05dc23cea1e63eed9c913424f"}] "usage" {"e-21423" [] "e-21429" [] "q-327781" [] "q-344824" [] "q-344659" []} "basis" 2570 "retractions" [[17592186167234 :question/problem "$y^{‴} - 5y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 8y = 6xe^{2x}, {y_{1} = e^{-x}, y_{2} = e^{2x}, y_{3} = e^{4x}}$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y_{p}(x) = u_{1}(x)e^{-x} + u_{2}(x)e^{2x} + u_{3}(x)e^{4x}$.\nWhat is the function $u_{2}(x)$?"] [17592186167105 :question/problem "$y^{‴} + y^{′} = \\sec x, {y_{1} = 1, y_{2} = \\cos (x), y_{3} = \\sin (x)}$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y(x) = A + B\\cos (x) + C\\sin (x) + u_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)\\cos (x) + u_{3}(x)\\sin (x)$.\nWhat is the function $u_{3}(x)$?"] [17592186167236 :question/problem "$y^{‴} + y^{′} = \\sec x\\tan x, {y_{1} = 1, y_{2} = \\cos (x), y_{3} = \\sin (x)}$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y(x) = A + B\\cos (x) + C\\sin (x) + u_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)\\cos (x) + u_{3}(x)\\sin (x)$.\nWhat is the function $u_{1}(x)$?"] [17592186166448 :question/problem "$y''' - 3y'' - 6y' + 8y = 2 + 5e^{-x}, \\qquad \\big\\{y_1 = e^{-2x}, \\: y_2 = e^{x}, \\: y_3 = e^{4x} \\big\\}$\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y_p(x) = u_1(x)e^{-2x} +u_2(x)e^{x} + u_3(x)e^{4x}$.\nWhat is the function $u_1(x)$?"] [17592186166448 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3}, \\qquad {u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int{{\\frac{{g(x){W_i}(x)}}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x}}, \\qquad i=1,2,3\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3$, and\n\n$$\nW_{1}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{2} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{2}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}, W_{2}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}, W_{3}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2 + 5e^{-x}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= e^{-2x}, \\qquad y_2= e^{x}, \\qquad y_3= e^{4x}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x) &= W(y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{-2x} & e^{x} & e^{4x} \\\\ -2e^{-2x} & e^{x} & 4e^{4x} \\\\ 4e^{-2x} & e^{x} & 16e^{4x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (e^{-2x} \\cdot e^{x} \\cdot e^{4x})\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 16\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{3x}[1(16 - 4) - 1(-32 - 16) + 1(-2 - 4)] \\\\\n&= e^{3x}(12 + 48 - 6) \\\\\n&= 54e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, to find $u_1$, we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{1}(x) &= W_{1}(y_{2}, y_{3}) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}y_{2} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{2}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{x} & e^{4x} \\\\ e^{x} & 4e^{4x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4e^{5x} - e^{5x} \\\\\n&= 3e^{5x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= (-1)^{3 - 1}∫(g(x)W_{1}(x))/(W(x))dx \\\\\n&= ∫((2 + 5e^{-x})(3e^{5x}))/(54e^{3x})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}∫(2 + 5e^{-x})e^{2x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}∫(2e^{2x} + 5e^{x})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}(e^{2x} + 5e^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166449 :question/problem "$y''' + 4y' = 6, \\qquad \\big\\{y_1 = 1, \\: y_2 = \\cos(2x), \\: y_3 = \\sin(2x) \\big\\}$\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y(x) = A + B \\cos(2x) + C \\sin(2x) + u_1(x) + u_2(x) \\cos(2x) + u_3(x) \\sin(2x)$.\nWhat is the function $u_3(x)$?"] [17592186166449 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3}, \\qquad {u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int \\frac{g(x){W_i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x, \\qquad i=1,2,3\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3$, and\n\n$$\nW_{1}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{2} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{2}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}, W_{2}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}, W_{3}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x) = 6$. Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= 1, \\qquad y_2= \\cos(2x), \\qquad y_3= \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x) &= W(y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\cos (2x) & \\sin (2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\sin (2x) & 2\\cos (2x) \\\\ 0 & -4\\cos (2x) & -4\\sin (2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}-2\\sin (2x) & 2\\cos (2x) \\\\ -4\\cos (2x) & -4\\sin (2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\sin^{2} (2x) + 8\\cos^{2} (2x) \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, to find $u_3$, we compute the determinant $W_3(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{3}(x) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\cos (2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\sin (2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2\\sin (2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{3}(x) &= (-1)^{3 - 3}∫(g(x)W_{3}(x))/(W(x))dx \\\\\n&= ∫(6(-2\\sin (2x)))/(8)dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}∫\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\\cos (2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164008 17592186164009 17592186164010 17592186164011 17592186164012 17592186164959 17592186164960 17592186164961 17592186164962 17592186164963 17592186164971 17592186164972 17592186164973 17592186164974 17592186164975] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164007 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164007 :answer-field/id] [17592186164007 :answer-field/key] [17592186164007 :answer-field/type] [17592186164958 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164958 :answer-field/id] [17592186164958 :answer-field/key] [17592186164958 :answer